{"alignments":[{"id":"alignment:c42f94cf540df5b1238a7743","layer":"CN","nodeId":"cnm_h071","reason":"Canonical description and mastery criteria are unchanged from this pinned native topic; placement remains a separate source annotation.","relation":"exact","reviewProvenance":{"comparedFields":["description","criteria"],"reviewMeaning":"Definition identity, not an official curriculum equivalence claim.","sourceRevision":"62b341d4b66b175413d6e3f697e37639fd96aa2d","type":"source_identity_comparison"},"reviewStatus":"machine_content_review","sourceNodeId":"cnm_h071","status":"accepted"}],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":0,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c42f94cf540df5b1238a7743"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B. sin x","reason":"sin(x−y)cos y+cos(x−y)sin y=sin((x−y)+y)=sin x.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h071"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1983_AMC_12_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"sine_addition","label":"Recognize the sine addition formula","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sine_addition":["cnm_h071"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h071"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c42f94cf540df5b1238a7743"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: sinα cosβ + cosα sinβ","reason":"The addition formula is sin(α + β) = sinα cosβ + cosα sinβ.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h071"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h071_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h060"],"id":"m1","label":"Recognize the sine addition formula","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_h071"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h071","cnm_h060"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c42f94cf540df5b1238a7743"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. tan15°","reason":"The numerator is 2 sin15° cos5° and the denominator is 2 cos15° cos5°. The common nonzero factor cancels, leaving tan15°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h071"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1984_AMC_12_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_276"],"id":"expand-about-average-angle","label":"Expand about the average angle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"expand-about-average-angle":["cnm_h071","cnm_h070"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h071","cnm_h070","cnm_276"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c42f94cf540df5b1238a7743"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 1/3","reason":"The squared sums give 5/3+1=2+2(sin a sin b+cos a cos b)=2+2cos(a-b). Therefore cos(a-b)=1/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h071"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_12A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"id":"square-and-add","label":"Square and add the equations","nodeIds":["cnm_h062","cnm_h071","cnm_h064"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_148"],"id":"solution:square-and-add:9b1370ff96d5","label":"Square and add the two equations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"square-and-add":["cnm_h062","cnm_h071"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h062","cnm_h071","cnm_148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c42f94cf540df5b1238a7743"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D (1/32)","reason":"For theta=2k*pi/7 with k=1,2,3, the identity 1+2(cos(theta)+cos(2theta)+cos(3theta))=0 gives 8x^3+4x^2-4x-1=0 for x=cos(theta). Hence a=1/2, b=-1/2, c=-1/8, and abc=1/32.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h071"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_12A_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"telescoping-trigonometric-sum","label":"Derive a cosine polynomial","methodDirectNodeIds":{"telescoping-trigonometric-sum":["cnm_h071","cnm_h073","math:telescoping"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h071","cnm_h073","math:telescoping"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cosine-symmetric-sums","label":"Use cosine identities and Vieta","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cosine-symmetric-sums":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h071","cnm_h073"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","cnm_h071","cnm_h073"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c42f94cf540df5b1238a7743"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 0≤y≤π","reason":"Apply the printed generic h070 difference identity at (alpha,beta)=(pi-x,y), then use sin(pi-u)=sin(u) and cos(pi-u)=-cos(u) to derive sin(x+y)=sin(x)*cos(y)+cos(x)*sin(y). For x,y in [0,pi], both sines are nonnegative and both cosines are at most 1, so the right side is at most sin(x)+sin(y). Every y in [0,pi] works, E. Actual h071 and h074 are numerically scoped, but this complete elementary route reuses an explicitly printed generic formula, as allowed by the separated D symbolic-formula policy. Do not equate this local derivation with general mastery of those numerical application nodes or invent a new synthesis target.","recordedAnswer":"E","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h071"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12A_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"bound-sine-addition","label":"Bound the sine addition formula","methodDirectNodeIds":{"bound-sine-addition":["cnm_h071","cnm_h018","cnm_h061","cnm_h068"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h071","cnm_h018","cnm_h061","cnm_h068"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c42f94cf540df5b1238a7743"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. −2","reason":"Setting b=0 shows that either f is identically zero or f(0)=1. In the latter case, a=b=1/2 gives f(1)=2f(1/2)^2−1≥−1, excluding −2. Values 0, 1, and −1 occur for f≡0, f≡1, and f(x)=cos(πx). Value 2 occurs for f(x)=(r^x+r^(−x))/2 with r=2+√3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h071"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_12B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"equal-inputs-and-attainable-examples","label":"Create a square","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-inputs-and-attainable-examples":["math:functional-equations","cnm_h071","math:real-exponent-laws"]},"nodeIds":["math:functional-equations","cnm_h071","math:real-exponent-laws"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c42f94cf540df5b1238a7743"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: -cosα","reason":"Supplementary-angle reflection reverses the horizontal coordinate, giving cos(180° - α) = -cosα.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h071"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_h064_q2","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cosine-difference","label":"Use the cosine difference identity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cosine-difference":["cnm_h071","cnm_h061"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h071","cnm_h061"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c42f94cf540df5b1238a7743"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. 18°","reason":"The equation is equivalent to cos2x cos3x−sin2x sin3x=cos5x=0. For x=18°, 5x=90°, so the equality holds.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h071"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1984_AMC_12_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h060","cnm_039"],"id":"cosine-addition","label":"Combine the products into one cosine","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cosine-addition":["cnm_h071"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h071","cnm_h060","cnm_039"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c42f94cf540df5b1238a7743"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: π/6","reason":"Write x=π/2+kπ and x+z=±π/3+2mπ. Consequently z is congruent to π/6 or 5π/6 modulo π. The least positive value is π/6, attained with x=−π/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h071"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2006_AMC_12A_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cosine-addition-identity","label":"Use the cosine addition identity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cosine-addition-identity":["cnm_h071","cnm_h062","cnm_h066"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h071","cnm_h062","cnm_h066"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c42f94cf540df5b1238a7743"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, -1","reason":"With t=tan x, tan(2023x)=Im((1+it)^2023)/Re((1+it)^2023). The denominator has constant term 1. The numerator's t^2023 coefficient is Im(i^2023)=-1, since 2023 is congruent to 3 modulo 4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h071"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_12A_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"tangent-addition-recursion","label":"Repeated tangent addition","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-addition-recursion":["cnm_h072","cnm_h071","cnm_h200"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h072","cnm_h200","cnm_h071"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c42f94cf540df5b1238a7743"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 2","reason":"Writing s = tan A + tan B and p = tan A tan B gives 1 = tan45° = s/(1 - p). Thus s + p = 1 and (1 + tan A)(1 + tan B) = 1 + s + p = 2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h071"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1985_AMC_12_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h071","cnm_276","cnm_277","cnm_146"],"id":"tangent-addition","label":"Use the 45-degree sum","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-addition":["cnm_h072"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h072","cnm_h071","cnm_276","cnm_277","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c42f94cf540df5b1238a7743"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (30°)","reason":"Squaring and adding gives 37=25+24sin(A+B), so sin C=1/2. Thus C is 30° or 150°. If C=150°, then A<30° and 3sin A+4cos B<5.5, contradicting its value 6. Therefore C=30°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h071"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1999_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"square-and-add-trigonometric-equations","label":"Square and add","methodDirectNodeIds":{"square-and-add-trigonometric-equations":["cnm_h062","cnm_h071","cnm_148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h062","cnm_h071","cnm_148"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h060","cnm_277","cnm_110"],"id":"square-and-add","label":"Square and add","methodDirectNodeIds":{"square-and-add":["cnm_115","cnm_h064","cnm_h062","cnm_h071","cnm_148","cnm_h067","cnm_h068"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_115","cnm_h064","cnm_h062","cnm_h071","cnm_148","cnm_h067","cnm_h068","cnm_h060","cnm_277","cnm_110"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c42f94cf540df5b1238a7743"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (-4)","reason":"On0<x<pi, sin(x)>0. Derive the sine-addition identity from the explicitly printed sine-difference formula using pi reductions, then obtain sin(2x)=2sin(x)cos(x) and sin(3x)=sin(x)*(4cos(x)^2-1), using the unit-circle coordinate identity sin^2+cos^2=1. Put t=cos(x). The cosine function is strictly decreasing from 1 to-1 on(0,pi): write cos(x)=sin(pi/2-x) and use the represented sine monotonicity on(-pi/2,pi/2). Thus each t in (-1,1) gives exactly one admissible x. Divide by positive sin(x) and factor a*(1+2t)=4t^2-1 as(2t+1)*(2t-1-a)=0. The root t=-1/2 is always admissible. The other root (a+1)/2 is admissible exactly when-3<a<1 and is different from-1/2 exactly when a!=-2. Hence more than one solution occurs for (-3,-2) union(-2,1), giving p+q+r=-4, A. Endpoint t=+/-1 and coincident roots are excluded explicitly.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h071"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factor-in-cosine","label":"Factor in cosine","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-in-cosine":["cnm_h071","cnm_h073","cnm_h068","cnm_224","cnm_099"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h071","cnm_h073","cnm_h068","cnm_224","cnm_099"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"shared-sine-factor","label":"Expose the shared sine factor","methodDirectNodeIds":{"shared-sine-factor":["cnm_h070","cnm_h071","cnm_h073","cnm_h062","cnm_h068","cnm_099"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h070","cnm_h071","cnm_h073","cnm_h062","cnm_h068","cnm_099"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c42f94cf540df5b1238a7743"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 150°","reason":"Let the equal sides be s and the base angles be α<45°. Then b=2s cos α and h=s sin α, so s^2=2bh gives 1=4 sin α cos α=2 sin(2α). Since 0<2α<90°, 2α=30°, and the vertex angle is 180°-30°=150°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h071"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_12B_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"altitude-sine-addition","label":"Altitude and sine addition","methodDirectNodeIds":{"altitude-sine-addition":["cnm_276","cnm_h071","cnm_h067","cnm_277"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_276","cnm_h071","cnm_h067","cnm_277"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c42f94cf540df5b1238a7743"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B. 1/2","reason":"Put c=cos36° and d=cos72°. Expanding sin108°=sin72° and using sin72°=2sin36°c gives 2c=2c²+d. Also d=2c²-1, so 4c²-2c-1=0 and d=c-1/2. Therefore c-d=1/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h071"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1975_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h060","cnm_h061","cnm_040"],"id":"supplementary-sine-relation","label":"Connect the angles through their sines","methodDirectNodeIds":{"supplementary-sine-relation":["cnm_h074","cnm_h071","cnm_h073","cnm_h064"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h074","cnm_h071","cnm_h073","cnm_h064","cnm_h060","cnm_h061","cnm_040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c42f94cf540df5b1238a7743"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 399","reason":"There are 10 relevant negative lobes on [1/20,1] and 190 positive lobes on [1,20]. Each has two intersections, including x=1 where applicable. That shared root is counted twice, giving 2(10+190)-1=399.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h071"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2025_AMC_12B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"half-wave-intersections","label":"Count compatible sine lobes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"half-wave-intersections":["cnm_h049","cnm_h075","cnm_h047","cnm_h066","cnm_h067","cnm_h071","cnm_h021"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h075","cnm_h066","cnm_h047","cnm_h049","cnm_h067","cnm_h071","cnm_h021"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c42f94cf540df5b1238a7743"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 12","reason":"The area is (n/2)R^2 sin(360°/n). For n=12 this is 6R^2 sin30°=3R^2, giving choice C.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h071"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1961_AMC_12_Q32","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_276","cnm_h060","cnm_211"],"id":"central-triangle-area","label":"Split the polygon into central triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"central-triangle-area":["cnm_266","cnm_p148","cnm_277","cnm_h070","cnm_h071"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_266","cnm_p148","cnm_277","cnm_h070","cnm_h071","cnm_276","cnm_h060","cnm_211"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c42f94cf540df5b1238a7743"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. 0","reason":"Put z=cos(x)+i sin(x), so |z|=1 and F(z)=z^3-z^2+z+1. If F(z)=0, conjugating and multiplying by z^3 gives z^3+z^2-z+1=0. Subtraction yields 2z(z-1)=0. Since z≠0, this forces z=1, but F(1)=2. Thus there are no such x.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h071"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_Fall_AMC_12B_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h064","cnm_h173","cnm_h066","cnm_h068"],"id":"real-and-imaginary-cases","label":"Separate real and imaginary parts","methodDirectNodeIds":{"real-and-imaginary-cases":["cnm_h099","cnm_h071","cnm_h070","cnm_h060"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h070","cnm_h064","cnm_h060","cnm_h173","cnm_h066","cnm_h068","cnm_h099","cnm_h071"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c42f94cf540df5b1238a7743"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 147","reason":"Let ζ=e^(2πi/7). For m=±1,±2, the sum of ζ^(mk) over k=1,...,6 is -1. Since |1-z|⁴=6-4(z+z⁻¹)+z²+z⁻² for |z|=1, the six chords from one vertex contribute 36+8-2=42. Summing over seven vertices counts each chord twice, giving 7×42/2=147.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h071"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_266","cnm_h060","cnm_h062","cnm_h070","cnm_h071","cnm_h173"],"id":"finite_cosine_sums","label":"Expand the fourth powers and sum","methodDirectNodeIds":{"finite_cosine_sums":["cnm_h073","math:telescoping","math:double-counting"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h073","math:telescoping","math:double-counting","cnm_266","cnm_h060","cnm_h062","cnm_h070","cnm_h071","cnm_h173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":10,"alignmentIds":["alignment:c42f94cf540df5b1238a7743"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, pi^2/6","reason":"The same-angle identity and cosine addition formulas give sin(x)^2+sin(y)^2=1-cos(x+y)cos(x-y) and sin(x)sin(y)=(cos(x-y)-cos(x+y))/2. Therefore the difference from 3/4 is (1/2-cos(x-y))*(1/2+cos(x+y)). Monotonic sine on its principal interval and cofunction identities justify the cosine thresholds; this is not inferred from a sketch. On the square, put p=x+y,q=x-y: 0<=p<=pi, |q|<=pi/2 and p+|q|<=pi. The nonpositive product gives |q|<=pi/3 and p<=2pi/3; the opposite sign region adds only the two boundary points. Removing two triangles of leg pi/6 and one of leg pi/3 from area pi^2/4 gives pi^2/6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h071"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2006_AMC_12B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h062","cnm_277","cnm_p089","cnm_p148"],"id":"complete-square-and-sine-bounds","label":"Complete the square to reveal straight boundary lines","methodDirectNodeIds":{"complete-square-and-sine-bounds":["math:completing-square-expression","cnm_h070","cnm_h071","cnm_h064","cnm_h067","math:linear-half-plane-systems","cnm_p150"]},"nodeIds":["math:completing-square-expression","cnm_h070","cnm_h071","cnm_h064","cnm_h067","math:linear-half-plane-systems","cnm_p150","cnm_h062","cnm_277","cnm_p089","cnm_p148"]}]}],"canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_h071"],"label":"两角和的正弦与余弦","layer":"CN","meaning":"Practice covers aligned canonical concepts, with no claim of complete standard coverage or mastery.","nextOffset":null,"sourceNode":{"criteria":["能计算 sin(α+β) 在特殊角上的值","能区分和角与差角公式的符号"],"description":"会用和角公式计算能化成 0、±1/2、±1 的值。","id":"cnm_h071","label":"两角和的正弦与余弦","original":{"chapter":"高一 第五章 三角函数 5.5","description":"会用和角公式计算能化成 0、±1/2、±1 的值。","domain":"数与代数","external":false,"grade":10,"id":"cnm_h071","mastery_criteria":["能计算 sin(α+β) 在特殊角上的值","能区分和角与差角公式的符号"],"name":"两角和的正弦与余弦","semester":"上","standards":[{"confidence":"inferred","id":"cms-2017:H.FUN.16","note":"和角公式与差角公式同属恒等变换。待核。"}],"type":"procedural"},"placement":{"bookCode":"10","bookLabel":"必修一","chapter":"高一 第五章 三角函数 5.5","domain":"数与代数","semester":"上"},"reviewStatus":"source_only"},"sourceNodeId":"cnm_h071","status":"mapped","total"